A Streamlit-based tracker for U.S. Congress stock transactions. It monitors publicly disclosed House and Senate trades, tracks selected politicians and their transactions, and provides alerts when new trades are reported.

Data pipeline
-------------
fetch_trades.py runs daily (see .github/workflows/fetch-trades.yml) and pulls
from a free, keyless, no-rate-limit feed (kadoa-org/congress-trading-monitor's
public/data/trades.json, sourced from official House Clerk / Senate eFD /
OGE filings). It writes the merged dataset to data/trades.json, which
index.html / app.js reads directly. Search always runs over the full
dataset; the table just paginates the matches 50 at a time so it stays
fast to render. The "Top 5 Whale Trades" dashboard highlights the largest
disclosed trades (by dollar amount) from the last 30 days.

Email alerts
------------
Visitors manage their own subscription entirely from the "Email Alerts" tab -
no GitHub tab opens, and subscriber emails are never stored anywhere in this
repo (see architecture below).

Subscribe: pick one or more politicians, enter an email, hit Subscribe. The
site POSTs directly to a small Cloudflare Worker, which validates the
request (real-looking email, every politician must match a filer name
already in data/trades.json), emails a verification link to that address,
and only records a *pending* request (under a random, single-use, 48-hour
token) - nothing is active yet. Clicking the link in the email hits the
Worker again, which moves the request into confirmed state and shows a
plain confirmation page naming exactly which politicians are now tracked.
A subscription can only activate if whoever submitted the form also
controls that inbox.

Unsubscribe: same tab, same politician picker (or an "unsubscribe from all"
checkbox). This POSTs straight to the Worker and takes effect immediately,
no confirmation email - anyone who knows an address can turn its alerts
off, which is low-harm (worst case someone has to resubscribe).

Architecture: the Worker (cloudflare-worker/) stores subscriptions in
Cloudflare KV, not in this git repo - so nobody, including the repo owner,
sees subscriber emails just by looking at the codebase. The daily
fetch-trades.yml workflow calls the Worker's private /alert-recipients
endpoint (a shared API key, not the public site) to get the current
confirmed list, matches it against newly-seen trades, and sends the actual
alert emails via Brevo. Email sending (both the verification link and the
trade alerts) goes through Brevo's HTTP API rather than SMTP, because
Cloudflare Workers can't do raw SMTP - both the Worker and send_alerts.py
just make a plain HTTPS POST to Brevo, no SMTP libraries involved
anywhere. (Originally used SendGrid, but SendGrid retired its permanent
free tier in mid-2025 - Brevo's free plan, 300 emails/day forever with no
card, is the closest match: same single-sender-by-email-link verification,
no domain/DNS needed.)

Deploying the Worker (one-time setup):

1. Create a free Cloudflare account (no credit card) and install the CLI:
   `npm install -g wrangler` (or use `npx wrangler` as below without
   installing globally).

2. From cloudflare-worker/, create the KV namespace and paste the id it
   prints into wrangler.toml's `kv_namespaces` entry (replacing
   REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KV_NAMESPACE_ID):

     npx wrangler login
     npx wrangler kv namespace create SUBS_KV

3. Create a free Brevo account (brevo.com), go to Senders, Domains & Dedicated
   IPs -> Senders -> add your own email as a sender and verify it via the
   code/link Brevo emails you (no domain needed), then go to Settings ->
   API Keys and generate one.

4. Set the Worker's secrets (from cloudflare-worker/):

     npx wrangler secret put BREVO_API_KEY
     npx wrangler secret put FROM_EMAIL              # the address you verified in step 3
     npx wrangler secret put SUBSCRIPTIONS_API_KEY    # any long random string you make up

5. Deploy: `npx wrangler deploy`. It prints the Worker's URL
   (something like https://congress-stock-tracker-alerts.<subdomain>.workers.dev).

6. Paste that URL into the `ALERTS_WORKER_URL` constant at the top of
   app.js and push - the Email Alerts tab is now live.

7. Add these repository secrets (Settings -> Secrets and variables ->
   Actions) so fetch-trades.yml's alert step can reach the same Worker and
   Brevo account:

   - ALERTS_WORKER_URL     the Worker URL from step 5
   - SUBSCRIPTIONS_API_KEY the same value you set in step 4
   - BREVO_API_KEY         the same key from step 3
   - FROM_EMAIL            the same verified sender from step 3

Without those four repository secrets set, send_alerts.py just skips
sending mail (the daily workflow still runs and updates trades.json
normally) - and without ALERTS_WORKER_URL set in app.js, the Email Alerts
tab tells visitors it isn't set up yet instead of silently failing.
